## Solution Steps

### 1. Initial Reconnaissance

I started by downloading the two files and listing the directory to confirm what was given:

```bash
$ ls -la
total 36
drwxr-xr-x 2 user user  4096 Sep  7 12:00 .
drwxr-xr-x 3 user user  4096 Sep  7 12:00 ..
-rw-r--r-- 1 user user  8408 Sep  7 12:00 ltdis.sh
-rw-r--r-- 1 user user 16712 Sep  7 12:00 static


The file static is clearly a binary, while ltdis.sh is a shell script. I checked what kind of binary static i

$ file static
static: ELF 64-bit LSB pie executable, x86-64, version 1 (SYSV), dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, for GNU/Linux 3.2.0, BuildID[sha1]=..., not stripped

$ cat ltdis.sh
#!/bin/bash
# ltdis.sh - a simple script to run common static analysis tools on a binary

echo "Attempting disassembly of $1 ..."
objdump -D "$1" > "$1.ltdis.x86_64.txt"

echo "Ripping strings from binary ..."
strings "$1" > "$1.ltdis.strings.txt"

echo "Done!"
i then search the flag using this comman "$ grep picoCTF static.ltdis.strings.txt" to see the flag
